your new record. You land in the Permian

261
00:09:43,577 --> 00:09:47,217
era, about 255 million years ago. Every

262
00:09:47,217 --> 00:09:49,377
continent has crashed together into one

263
00:09:49,377 --> 00:09:51,777
giant landmass called Pangaea, and

264
00:09:51,777 --> 00:09:54,257
you're standing in its hot, dry middle.

265
00:09:54,257 --> 00:09:55,977
And for the first time on this trip,

266
00:09:55,977 --> 00:09:57,897
there's a land animal here big enough to

267
00:09:57,897 --> 00:10:00,577
hunt you. The air is fine here, and fire

268
00:10:00,577 --> 00:10:02,137
is easy because there are plenty of

269
00:10:02,137 --> 00:10:04,617
trees. Water is harder to find because

270
00:10:04,617 --> 00:10:07,057
the middle of Pangaea is so far from any

271
00:10:07,057 --> 00:10:09,577
ocean that rain barely reaches it. And

272
00:10:09,577 --> 00:10:12,498
your food is meat or nothing. The plants

273
00:10:12,498 --> 00:10:14,738
here are pine trees and ferns, and your

274
00:10:14,738 --> 00:10:16,978
body can't digest them. There's still no

275
00:10:16,978 --> 00:10:19,538
fruit anywhere on Earth. So, you catch

276
00:10:19,538 --> 00:10:21,458
small reptiles and whatever else you can

277
00:10:21,458 --> 00:10:23,338
grab, and you stay close to the few

278
00:10:23,338 --> 00:10:25,338
rivers you can find. At one of those

279
00:10:25,338 --> 00:10:27,378
rivers, you spot a predator about as

280
00:10:27,378 --> 00:10:29,738
long as a tiger with a narrow head and a

281
00:10:29,738 --> 00:10:32,178
long low body. Your first thought might

282
00:10:32,178 --> 00:10:33,418
be that you could fight it off with a

283
00:10:33,418 --> 00:10:36,538
sharp stick. Then, you see its teeth.

284
00:10:36,538 --> 00:10:39,058
Two fangs hang from its top jaw, and

285
00:10:39,058 --> 00:10:41,578
each one is about as long as a pen. It's

286
00:10:41,578 --> 00:10:44,898
called Inostrancevia. In the late 1800s,

287
00:10:44,898 --> 00:10:47,018
a Russian scientist dug two almost

288
00:10:47,018 --> 00:10:48,978
complete skeletons of it out of a river

289
00:10:48,978 --> 00:10:50,938
bank in northern Russia. It was the

290
00:10:50,938 --> 00:10:52,978
biggest hunter of its kind, and it

291
00:10:52,978 --> 00:10:54,898
killed with slashing bites from those

292
00:10:54,898 --> 00:10:57,618
fangs. And this animal isn't a reptile,

293
00:10:57,618 --> 00:10:59,938
and it isn't a dinosaur. It belongs to

294
00:10:59,938 --> 00:11:01,378
the branch of the family tree that

295
00:11:01,378 --> 00:11:03,978
eventually leads to mammals like you.

296
00:11:03,978 --> 00:11:05,938
So, the first land animal on this trip

297
00:11:05,938 --> 00:11:08,018
that can hunt you is your very distant

298
00:11:08,018 --> 00:11:10,138
cousin. One afternoon, you're carrying a

299
00:11:10,138 --> 00:11:12,418
small reptile back to your camp, and an

300
00:11:12,418 --> 00:11:14,618
Inostrancevia charges out from behind a

301
00:11:14,618 --> 00:11:16,698
rock. It's faster than you, and there's

302
00:11:16,698 --> 00:11:19,618
nothing to climb. You lasted two weeks,

303
00:11:19,618 --> 00:11:21,538
which is a big step backward. The

304
00:11:21,538 --> 00:11:23,698
machine pulls you out, and this time it

305
00:11:23,698 --> 00:11:26,458
only jumps 3 million years. You land at

306
00:11:26,458 --> 00:11:28,418
the very start of the Triassic era,

307
00:11:28,418 --> 00:11:31,258
about 252 million years ago, in the

308
00:11:31,258 --> 00:11:33,138
middle of the worst disaster in the

309
00:11:33,138 --> 00:11:35,458
history of life on Earth. Scientists

310
00:11:35,458 --> 00:11:38,458
call it the Great Dying. Inside Siberia,

311
00:11:38,458 --> 00:11:40,738
the ground has split open, and volcanoes

312
00:11:40,738 --> 00:11:42,458
there have been pouring out lava for

313
00:11:42,458 --> 00:11:44,818
hundreds of thousands of years. They've

314
00:11:44,818 --> 00:11:46,898
pumped so much carbon dioxide into the

315
00:11:46,898 --> 00:11:49,178
air that the whole planet is heating up,

316
00:11:49,178 --> 00:11:50,898
like a car parked in the sun with the

317
00:11:50,898 --> 00:11:53,258
windows shut. Around nine out of every

318
00:11:53,258 --> 00:11:55,138
10 kinds of animals in the ocean are

319
00:11:55,138 --> 00:11:57,138
dying out. Most people think the

320
00:11:57,138 --> 00:11:58,858
deadliest time in Earth's history was

321
00:11:58,858 --> 00:12:00,978
the age of the dinosaurs. It's actually

322
00:12:00,978 --> 00:12:02,858
this one, and there isn't a single

323
00:12:02,858 --> 00:12:05,378
dinosaur in it. A team of scientists dug

324
00:12:05,378 --> 00:12:08,538
about 15,000 tiny fossil teeth out of

325
00:12:08,538 --> 00:12:11,058
two tons of rock in South China. The

326
00:12:11,058 --> 00:12:13,459
teeth came from small eel-like animals

327
00:12:13,459 --> 00:12:15,459
that lived in the sea. The chemistry in

328
00:12:15,459 --> 00:12:17,219
those teeth works like a thermometer

329
00:12:17,219 --> 00:12:19,259
from the past. It showed that the sea

330
00:12:19,259 --> 00:12:22,179
near the equator reached about 40° C,

331
00:12:22,179 --> 00:12:25,019
as hot as a hot bath. On land, it was

332
00:12:25,019 --> 00:12:28,459
probably even worse, around 50 to 60°.

333
00:12:28,459 --> 00:12:30,539
Almost every fish disappeared from the

334
00:12:30,539 --> 00:12:32,739
tropics, and life there didn't come back

335
00:12:32,739 --> 00:12:35,179
for about 5 million years. And the

336
00:12:35,179 --> 00:12:36,699
machine dropped you right in the

337
00:12:36,699 --> 00:12:39,019
tropics. There's no shade because most

338
00:12:39,019 --> 00:12:41,259
of the forests are dead. Your body cools

339
00:12:41,259 --> 00:12:43,379
itself by sweating, but in heat like

340
00:12:43,379 --> 00:12:45,539
this, sweating can't keep up. Your

341
00:12:45,539 --> 00:12:47,299
temperature keeps climbing, and there's

342
00:12:47,299 --> 00:12:49,539
nowhere cooler to go. The animals that

343
00:12:49,539 --> 00:12:51,339
survived this did it by hiding

344
00:12:51,339 --> 00:12:53,859
underground. Scientists scanned a fossil

345
00:12:53,859 --> 00:12:55,899
burrow from South Africa with a powerful

346
00:12:55,899 --> 00:12:57,659
x-ray machine, and they found two

347
00:12:57,659 --> 00:12:59,859
animals curled up inside it. One was a

348
00:12:59,859 --> 00:13:01,859
small digger, a distant relative of

349
00:13:01,859 --> 00:13:03,939
mammals like you. The other was an

350
00:13:03,939 --> 00:13:06,059
injured amphibian with broken ribs that

351
00:13:06,059 --> 00:13:08,459
had crawled in to rest. A flash flood

352
00:13:08,459 --> 00:13:10,419
buried them both, and they stayed down

353
00:13:10,419 --> 00:13:13,899
there for 250 million years. You can't

354
00:13:13,899 --> 00:13:15,539
dig a burrow that deep with your bare

355
00:13:15,539 --> 00:13:18,019
hands. You lasted about a day, your

356
00:13:18,019 --> 00:13:20,379
worst run since the Archean. The button

357
00:13:20,379 --> 00:13:22,099
lights up, and the machine jumps you

358
00:13:22,099 --> 00:13:24,379
about 100 million years forward. You

359
00:13:24,379 --> 00:13:27,139
land in the Jurassic era, about 150

360
00:13:27,139 --> 00:13:29,499
million years ago. Life has recovered,

361
00:13:29,499 --> 00:13:31,139
and the dinosaurs that first showed up

362
00:13:31,139 --> 00:13:32,979
in the late Triassic have taken over the

363
00:13:32,979 --> 00:13:35,299
planet. Breathing, drinking, and making

364
00:13:35,299 --> 00:13:37,779
fire are all easy now. Food is the

365
00:13:37,779 --> 00:13:39,979
problem again. Flowering plants haven't

366
00:13:39,979 --> 00:13:41,819
spread across the world yet, so there's

367
00:13:41,819 --> 00:13:44,499
still no fruit. You live on fish, eggs,

368
00:13:44,499 --> 00:13:45,979
and whatever small animals you can

369
00:13:45,979 --> 00:13:48,379
catch. The biggest hunter around here is

370
00:13:48,379 --> 00:13:51,059
Allosaurus. It's about 9 m long and

371
00:13:51,059 --> 00:13:53,139
heavier than a car, and it can run you

372
00:13:53,139 --> 00:13:55,299
down in the open. So, you sleep up in

373
00:13:55,299 --> 00:13:57,699
the trees because it can't climb. Then,

374
00:13:57,699 --> 00:13:59,579
one morning, you run out of eggs, and

375
00:13:59,579 --> 00:14:01,539
you have to climb down to find more.

376
00:14:01,539 --> 00:14:03,379
You're halfway across a clearing when an

377
00:14:03,379 --> 00:14:05,179
Allosaurus steps out of the trees in

378
00:14:05,179 --> 00:14:07,979
front of you. You lasted 2 weeks. The

379
00:14:07,979 --> 00:14:09,699
machine pulls you out and drops you

380
00:14:09,699 --> 00:14:12,139
about 80 million years later. You land

381
00:14:12,139 --> 00:14:14,699
in the Cretaceous era, about 68 million

382
00:14:14,699 --> 00:14:16,541
years ago. And for the first time on

383
00:14:16,541 --> 00:14:18,461
this whole trip, there's fruit on the

384
00:14:18,461 --> 00:14:20,581
trees. Flowering plants have spread

385
00:14:20,581 --> 00:14:22,861
across the world, and that changes

386
00:14:22,861 --> 00:14:25,061
everything for you. Farmers in northeast

387
00:14:25,061 --> 00:14:26,941
China once found one of the oldest

388
00:14:26,941 --> 00:14:29,301
flowering plants ever discovered pressed

389
00:14:29,301 --> 00:14:32,581
into a slab of rock. It's about 125

390
00:14:32,581 --> 00:14:35,181
million years old. Now, its relatives

391
00:14:35,181 --> 00:14:37,061
have covered the planet, and that means

392
00:14:37,061 --> 00:14:38,781
fruit and berries you can pick without

393
00:14:38,781 --> 00:14:41,341
hunting for every meal. So, food is

394
00:14:41,341 --> 00:14:43,501
finally solved. The problem is

395
00:14:43,501 --> 00:14:45,301
everything else that lives here.

396
00:14:45,301 --> 00:14:47,301
Tyrannosaurus Rex lives here, and it

397
00:14:47,301 --> 00:14:49,261
weighs more than an elephant. But, the

398
00:14:49,261 --> 00:14:51,221
animal that would really scare you isn't

399
00:14:51,221 --> 00:14:54,261
T-Rex. It's Quetzalcoatlus. Its wings

400
00:14:54,261 --> 00:14:56,701
stretch about 11 m across, and when it

401
00:14:56,701 --> 00:14:58,501
folds them and walks on the ground, it's

402
00:14:58,501 --> 00:15:00,821
as tall as a giraffe. Scientists think

403
00:15:00,821 --> 00:15:03,021
it stalked across open land like a giant

404
00:15:03,021 --> 00:15:05,741
stork, swallowing small animals whole.

405
00:15:05,741 --> 00:15:07,621
You're probably too big for it, but

406
00:15:07,621 --> 00:15:09,781
you'd never want to test that. So, you

407
00:15:09,781 --> 00:15:12,021
eat fruit and stay close to the forest.

408
00:15:12,021 --> 00:15:14,061
Then, one afternoon, you step out into

409
00:15:14,061 --> 00:15:15,901
the open to grab some berries, and a

410
00:15:15,901 --> 00:15:17,981
T-Rex is standing between you and the

411
00:15:17,981 --> 00:15:21,181
trees. You lasted 2 months. The machine

412
00:15:21,181 --> 00:15:22,941
pulls you out, and on the way forward,

413
00:15:22,941 --> 00:15:25,061
you fly right past the day an asteroid

414
00:15:25,061 --> 00:15:27,861
about 10 km wide hit what's now Mexico

415
00:15:27,861 --> 00:15:30,261
and ended the age of the dinosaurs. You

416
00:15:30,261 --> 00:15:32,741
land in the Paleogene era, about 58

417
00:15:32,741 --> 00:15:35,021
million years ago, and the dinosaurs are

418
00:15:35,021 --> 00:15:37,181
gone. Mammals are taking over the world

419
00:15:37,181 --> 00:15:39,541
now, and the whole planet is warm all

420
00:15:39,541 --> 00:15:41,941
the way up to the poles. Air, water,

421
00:15:41,941 --> 00:15:44,541
fire, and fruit are all easy here. So,

422
00:15:44,541 --> 00:15:46,221
the only danger left is what lives

423
00:15:46,221 --> 00:15:48,261
around you. The machine dropped you in a

424
00:15:48,261 --> 00:15:50,101
hot, swampy forest in what's now

425
00:15:50,101 --> 00:15:52,301
Colombia, and this swamp is home to the

426
00:15:52,301 --> 00:15:54,421
biggest snake that ever lived. It's

427
00:15:54,421 --> 00:15:57,061
called Titanoboa. Its bones were dug out

428
00:15:57,061 --> 00:15:59,301
of a coal mine right here in Colombia.

429
00:15:59,301 --> 00:16:01,701
It was about 13 m long and weighed over

430
00:16:01,701 --> 00:16:04,461
a ton, about as much as a small car. One

431
00:16:04,461 --> 00:16:06,141
of the scientists who studied it said it

432
00:16:06,141 --> 00:16:07,461
would have had to squeeze through his

433
00:16:07,461 --> 00:16:09,981
office door to get inside. It spent most

434
00:16:09,981 --> 00:16:11,501
of its time in the water hunting

435
00:16:11,501 --> 00:16:14,261
crocodiles. So, you stay on dry ground,

436
00:16:14,261 --> 00:16:15,941
away from the water, and you live on

437
00:16:15,941 --> 00:16:18,582
fruit. Then, the rainy season comes and

438
00:16:18,582 --> 00:16:21,022
the whole forest floods. One night, the

439
00:16:21,022 --> 00:16:22,582
water is up to your knees around your

440
00:16:22,582 --> 00:16:24,942
camp and a snake as thick as your waist

441
00:16:24,942 --> 00:16:27,422
is sliding toward you. You lasted 6

442
00:16:27,422 --> 00:16:29,822
months, your best run so far. The

443
00:16:29,822 --> 00:16:31,462
machine pulls you out and throws you

444
00:16:31,462 --> 00:16:34,022
about 50 million years forward. You land

445
00:16:34,022 --> 00:16:36,142
in the Miocene era, about 9 million

446
00:16:36,142 --> 00:16:38,342
years ago, in what's now Spain, and the

447
00:16:38,342 --> 00:16:40,622
forests have opened up into grassland.

448
00:16:40,622 --> 00:16:42,622
Grass is spreading across the world in

449
00:16:42,622 --> 00:16:45,062
this era, and herds of early horses and

450
00:16:45,062 --> 00:16:47,222
antelope follow it. You eat fruit from

451
00:16:47,222 --> 00:16:49,102
the trees at the edge of the grassland,

452
00:16:49,102 --> 00:16:50,862
and you scavenge meat from whatever the

453
00:16:50,862 --> 00:16:53,142
predators leave behind. And there are a

454
00:16:53,142 --> 00:16:55,982
lot of predators. Saber-toothed cats the

455
00:16:55,982 --> 00:16:58,302
size of a tiger hunt these plains, and

456
00:16:58,302 --> 00:17:00,662
out in the open, you're easy to spot.

457
00:17:00,662 --> 00:17:02,702
Near Madrid, a mining company digging

458
00:17:02,702 --> 00:17:05,462
for clay in 1991 broke into a set of

459
00:17:05,462 --> 00:17:08,262
ancient pits packed with bones. Animals

460
00:17:08,262 --> 00:17:10,102
had fallen into these pits and couldn't

461
00:17:10,102 --> 00:17:12,382
climb back out. Then predators came for

462
00:17:12,382 --> 00:17:14,382
the easy meal, jumped in, and got

463
00:17:14,382 --> 00:17:16,502
trapped, too. Scientists have pulled

464
00:17:16,502 --> 00:17:18,542
thousands of fossils out of these pits,

465
00:17:18,542 --> 00:17:20,182
and almost all of them belong to

466
00:17:20,182 --> 00:17:22,542
meat-eaters, including tiger-sized

467
00:17:22,542 --> 00:17:25,022
saber-toothed cats. One day, you find

468
00:17:25,022 --> 00:17:26,702
one of those pits, and there's a dead

469
00:17:26,702 --> 00:17:28,702
antelope lying at the bottom. It's the

470
00:17:28,702 --> 00:17:30,862
easiest meal you've seen in weeks, so

471
00:17:30,862 --> 00:17:33,142
you climb down. The walls are too steep

472
00:17:33,142 --> 00:17:35,502
to climb back up. A few hours later, a

473
00:17:35,502 --> 00:17:37,542
saber-toothed cat jumps in after the

474
00:17:37,542 --> 00:17:40,542
same meal. You lasted 10 months, almost

475
00:17:40,542 --> 00:17:42,862
a full year. The machine pulls you out

476
00:17:42,862 --> 00:17:44,702
of the pit and throws you about 6

477
00:17:44,702 --> 00:17:46,902
million years forward. You land in the

478
00:17:46,902 --> 00:17:49,462
Pliocene era, about 3 million years ago,

479
00:17:49,462 --> 00:17:51,502
in East Africa, and you see an ape

480
00:17:51,502 --> 00:17:53,822
walking on two legs. It's about as tall

481
00:17:53,822 --> 00:17:55,982
as a 7-year-old, with long arms and a

482
00:17:55,982 --> 00:17:58,302
small brain, and it walks upright across

483
00:17:58,302 --> 00:18:00,462
the grass, just like you do. It belongs

484
00:18:00,462 --> 00:18:02,702
to the same family as a famous skeleton

485
00:18:02,702 --> 00:18:06,382
named Lucy, found in Ethiopia in 1974.

486
00:18:06,382 --> 00:18:08,542
This land suits you better than any stop

487
00:18:08,542 --> 00:18:10,742
so far. Patches of woodland are mixed

488
00:18:10,742 --> 00:18:12,862
into the grassland, so you have fruit,

489
00:18:12,862 --> 00:18:15,542
nuts, shade, and water. And you already

490
00:18:15,542 --> 00:18:17,342
know how to make fire, but it's

491
00:18:17,342 --> 00:18:20,582
dangerous, even for them. In 1924,

492
00:18:20,582 --> 00:18:22,503
workers blasting rock at a quarry in

493
00:18:22,503 --> 00:18:24,263
South Africa found the skull of a

494
00:18:24,263 --> 00:18:26,783
3-year-old from Lucy's family. Decades

495
00:18:26,783 --> 00:18:29,023
later, a scientist found talon marks

496
00:18:29,023 --> 00:18:31,543
inside its eye sockets. An eagle had

497
00:18:31,543 --> 00:18:33,743
snatched that child. Lucy's family

498
00:18:33,743 --> 00:18:35,503
survives by staying in groups and

499
00:18:35,503 --> 00:18:37,263
watching out for each other. They won't

500
00:18:37,263 --> 00:18:39,703
let a stranger like you get close. So,

501
00:18:39,703 --> 00:18:41,743
you're on your own. And being alone

502
00:18:41,743 --> 00:18:43,663
means nobody watches the fire while you

503
00:18:43,663 --> 00:18:46,303
sleep. One night, it burns out and a

504
00:18:46,303 --> 00:18:49,503
leopard finds you. You lasted 3 years,

505
00:18:49,503 --> 00:18:52,183
your best run by far. The machine pulls

506
00:18:52,183 --> 00:18:54,023
you out one more time and throws you

507
00:18:54,023 --> 00:18:56,863
almost 3 million years forward. You land

508
00:18:56,863 --> 00:18:59,543
in the Pleistocene era, about 300,000

509
00:18:59,543 --> 00:19:02,023
years ago. This is the Ice Age, so

510
00:19:02,023 --> 00:19:03,743
you're expecting the worst stop of the

511
00:19:03,743 --> 00:19:05,823
whole trip. Ice covers a lot of the

512
00:19:05,823 --> 00:19:07,703
north, but the machine dropped you in

513
00:19:07,703 --> 00:19:10,103
North Africa and it's green here. Where

514
00:19:10,103 --> 00:19:11,943
the Sahara Desert is today, there's

515
00:19:11,943 --> 00:19:14,863
grassland, rivers, and herds of animals.

516
00:19:14,863 --> 00:19:17,143
A few weeks in, you catch a fever.

517
00:19:17,143 --> 00:19:19,143
You're too weak to find food or keep

518
00:19:19,143 --> 00:19:21,423
your fire going. One night, you're lying

519
00:19:21,423 --> 00:19:23,503
